00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:00.709 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  the verdict was swift and decisive  the case that became a national  referendum on racial justice still held  the power to surprise  guilty guilty guilty this verdict a jury  found former minneapolis police officer  derrick chauvin guilty in the racially  charged killing of george floyd  on all counts second degree murder third  degree murder  and second degree manslaughter today  you have the cameras all around the  world  to see and show what happened to my  brother  it was a motion picture the world seeing  his life being extinguished  and i could do nothing but watch  especially in that courtroom  over and over and over again as my  brother was murdered  there's no comparison i can't even  that's just i mean there's no words i  can't even compare  that to this because we got justice  we got just as we want okay  we won he may not be here  but we won it was chauvin  expressionless in court who was on trial  but it was floyd who became a symbol of  racial justice for black americans who  felt  targeted and abused by police for  generations  we always maintained the moral high  ground  knowing that we were on the right side  of history  the verdict resonated in the white house  where president joe biden  called floyd's family we hadn't seen  since the civil rights year in the 60s  protests that unified people of every  race and generation  in peace and with purpose to say enough  enough enough of the senseless killings  today today's verdict is  a step forward the legal system had to  operate behind barricades manned by  national guard troops  in a city that boarded up businesses and  closed its schools  in anticipation of a ruling that ended  months of demonstrations  with celebration the case that began  with a fake  twenty dollar bill and ended with a  nationwide movement  might have gone unnoticed if it hadn't  been for a viral video taken by a  seventeen-year-old girl  as excruciating to watch as it was to  re-watch  over and over again throughout the trial  of a man treated without dignity  speaking his final words  crowds have manned the streets of  minneapolis and the nation since floyd  died on may 25th  as they leave the courthouse square they  insist the movement  will continue john hendren al jazeera  minneapolis

